From 112cb9d6f45bfbe2c87829abec3680b4bfcd388d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yury Kirsanov <yury@kfi.net.au>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 10:42:53 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] mem: HG_MALLOC - hugepage-backed slab allocator with
 elastic arenas

A new shared- and private-memory allocator, selected at runtime with
-a HG_MALLOC (or -s/-k); nothing changes without it.

 - 21 size classes (64 B - 64 KB) served from per-process free lists, so
   the common allocation and free take no lock; cells move between
   processes through a shared per-class pool, in batches. Larger requests
   go to a coalescing large tier.
 - Blocks are carved from a buddy allocator inside each 2 MB page; a block
   whose cells are all free returns to the buddy and can be re-cut for any
   class.
 - The arena is acquired through a four-step ladder - MAP_HUGETLB, THP via
   MADV_HUGEPAGE, MADV_COLLAPSE, plain 4 KB pages - each tier verified from
   the kernel's counters. Huge pages are an optimisation, not a requirement.
 - Elastic arenas: -m INIT:CAP / -M INIT:CAP (k/m/g suffixes, bare numbers
   in MB) reserve the cap and commit pages on demand, in a configurable
   granule (shm_grow_granule / pkg_grow_granule), growing on exhaustion or
   ahead of it through an auto_scaling_profile (shm_/pkg_auto_scaling_profile),
   and releasing quiet pages. A maintenance process grows ahead of demand and
   runs the release with the arena lock dropped. Growth stops at the cap,
   the backing tier and a host free-memory floor (hg_ram_floor_mb); a
   sustained refusal latches GROW-BLOCKED, raised as E_CORE_SHM_GROW_BLOCKED.
 - Every grow is an mlock() of the new range, and mlock() checks
   RLIMIT_MEMLOCK on hugetlb as well: the arena warns at startup (and under
   -C) when the limit cannot cover its growth, and a refused grow names the
   resource that ran out - the limit or the hugetlb pool.
 - core:hg_stats (MI) per arena: tier, committed/reserved, per-class carve
   and reuse, grows/shrinks, lock hold/wait histograms per reason and the
   corruption counters; HG statistics next to shmem:; E_CORE_HG_LOCK_STALL
   for a hold above hg_lock_stall_us.
 - A per-block free map at the pool boundary makes a double free exact and
   stops a free-list walk at a scribbled link; both are counted and logged
   and the process keeps running.
 - mem/mem_arena.h: a module may ask for its own HG-managed arena,
   independent of the core allocator choice (stubs when HG is not built).

Builds clean with gcc and clang -Werror, with HG_MALLOC compiled out, on
arm32/arm64, i386 and musl. Design, configuration, deployment and test
procedures: mem/README.hg_malloc_v3.md.
